+# Hegel Property-Based Testing Report: glam
+
+Property-based tests written with `hegeltest` v0.28.2 (library crate name:
+`hegel`), added as a dev-dependency with
+`cargo add --dev hegeltest --ignore-rust-version` (glam pins
+`rust-version = 1.68.2`; hegeltest's MSRV is 1.86 — a maintainer decision if
+these tests are upstreamed).
+
+Note: the task brief mentioned an existing proptest dev-dependency, but glam
+0.33.2 has **no** proptest dependency and no existing property-based tests
+(`grep -ri proptest` over the repo is empty); the existing suite is entirely
+example-based `macro_rules!`-instantiated unit tests plus a 15-degree-grid
+euler sweep. The hegel tests below therefore supplement the unit suite rather
+than port anything.
+
+Tests live at the end of the existing integration-test files, in
+`mod hegel_props` / `impl_hegel_*` macro blocks gated
+`#[cfg(not(target_family = "wasm"))]` (hegel drives the native test runner):
+
+- `tests/vec3.rs` (3 properties + 1 deterministic bug pin)
+- `tests/vec4.rs` (2 properties)
+- `tests/mat3.rs` (2 properties)
+- `tests/mat4.rs` (2 properties × {`Mat4`, `DMat4`})
+- `tests/quat.rs` (3 properties × {`Quat`, `DQuat`})
+- `tests/affine3.rs` (3 properties)
+- `tests/euler.rs` (1 property × {`Quat`, `DQuat`})
+
+The highest-value angle for glam's architecture is the **SIMD-vs-scalar
+differential**: within one default build, `Vec3`/`Mat3` are always scalar
+while `Vec3A`/`Mat3A` use the SIMD backend (NEON here; SSE2/WASM elsewhere),
+so building both from identical components and comparing is a direct
+backend differential. `Vec4`/`Quat`/`Mat4`/`Affine3A` (SIMD, no scalar twin)
+are checked against hand-written scalar references on the components and
+against the always-scalar f64 types (`DVec4`, `DQuat`, `DMat4`, `DAffine3`)
+as higher-precision oracles. The full suite was additionally run with
+`--features scalar-math`, which swaps in the scalar backend for everything —
+a differential-by-rerun on top of the in-build differentials.
+
+## Properties tested
+
+| # | Test | Property | Evidence |
+|---|------|----------|----------|
+| 1 | `vec3.rs hegel_vec3_vec3a_lanewise_ops_agree_exactly` | `Vec3` (scalar) and `Vec3A` (SIMD) agree **exactly** on `+ - * min max abs neg` | Lane-wise IEEE ops have no reassociation freedom; lib.rs "SIMD and scalar consistency" section promises the types are interchangeable representations |
+| 2 | `vec3.rs hegel_vec3_and_vec3a_match_f64_oracle` | `dot`, `cross`, `length` of both `Vec3` and `Vec3A` match the `DVec3` f64 oracle within magnitude-calibrated tolerance | `as_dvec3` exists precisely for widening; the SIMD-divergence bug class from the property catalogue |
+| 3 | `vec3.rs hegel_vec3_normalize_family_contract` | For any input (incl. NaN/inf/huge/subnormal): `try_normalize` returns `None` or a unit vector; `normalize_or_zero` returns zero or a unit vector | Doc comments: "normalized to length 1.0 if possible, else returns `None`" / "else returns zero"; `is_normalized` documents its ~1e-4 threshold |
+| 4 | `vec4.rs hegel_vec4_lanewise_ops_match_scalar_reference` | SIMD `Vec4` lane-wise ops agree exactly with a hand-written scalar reference on the components | Fast-path-vs-generic oracle: the scalar backend computes exactly these expressions (src/f32/scalar/vec4.rs) |
+| 5 | `vec4.rs hegel_vec4_dot_length_match_f64_oracle` | `Vec4::dot`/`length` match the f64 oracle; `length_squared == dot(self, self)` exactly | `length_squared` doc: "the dot product of `self` with itself"; SIMD reduction-order divergence class |
+| 6 | `mat3.rs hegel_mat3_vs_mat3a_ops_agree` | `Mat3` vs `Mat3A` from the same 9 components: `transpose` exact; `mul_vec3`, `mat*mat`, `determinant` within element-magnitude tolerance | Same-crate SIMD/scalar twin types; ARCHITECTURE.md documents the storage-only difference |
+| 7 | `mat3.rs hegel_mat3_vs_mat3a_inverse_agree` | `Mat3::inverse` and `Mat3A::inverse` agree element-wise (scale `max\|m\|²/\|det\|`), for f64-verified-invertible matrices | Both backends implement adjugate/determinant; `inverse` doc: result invalid only if not invertible |
+| 8 | `mat4.rs hegel_mat4_inverse_roundtrip` (`Mat4` + `DMat4`) | For well-conditioned SRT matrices: `try_inverse` is `Some` and `m * m.inverse() ≈ IDENTITY` (tolerance scaled by `max\|m\|·max\|m⁻¹\|`) | `inverse` doc panics/invalid only for non-invertible input; existing unit tests check specific SRT instances |
+| 9 | `mat4.rs hegel_mat4_cols_array_roundtrip` (`Mat4` + `DMat4`) | `from_cols_array`/`to_cols_array`/`_2d` are exact roundtrips; `transpose` is an exact involution — full finite range incl. huge/subnormal | Conversions are pure memcpy/lane-shuffles; existing `test_const` checks one hardcoded array |
+| 10 | `quat.rs hegel_quat_from_axis_angle_unit_and_fixes_axis` (`Quat` + `DQuat`) | `from_axis_angle(unit_axis, any finite angle)` is normalized and leaves its own (scaled) axis fixed | `from_axis_angle` requires a normalized axis (glam_assert); sin²+cos²=1 for any finite angle; axis is the rotation's eigenvector |
+| 11 | `quat.rs hegel_quat_conjugate_is_inverse` (`Quat` + `DQuat`) | For unit q: `q * q.conjugate() ≈ IDENTITY` and `q.inverse() ≈ q.conjugate()` (32·eps) | `conjugate` doc: "equal to its inverse" if normalized; `inverse` doc: conjugate for unit quaternions |
+| 12 | `quat.rs hegel_quat_rotation_preserves_length_and_matches_mat3` (`Quat` + `DQuat`) | Unit-quat rotation preserves length; sandwich product agrees with `Mat3::from_quat(q).mul_vec3(v)` | Rotations are isometries; matrix path is an independently implemented sibling (existing `test_mul_vec3` checks 90° instances) |
+| 13 | `affine3.rs hegel_affine3a_matches_mat4_transform_point` | `Affine3A` and `Mat4` from the same SRT transform points identically (vs shared `DAffine3` f64 oracle); `project_point3` agrees for affine matrices | `Mat4::transform_point3` doc: "the equivalent of multiplying [...] as a 4D vector where `w` is `1.0`"; both types document the same SRT semantics |
+| 14 | `affine3.rs hegel_affine3a_composition_matches_sequential` | `(a * b).transform_point3(p) ≈ a.transform_point3(b.transform_point3(p))`, both vs the f64 oracle | Affine `Mul` doc/semantics; associativity of transform application |
+| 15 | `affine3.rs hegel_affine3a_inverse_roundtrips_points` | `a.inverse().transform_point3(a.transform_point3(p)) ≈ p` for well-conditioned SRT | `inverse` doc: "Note that if the transformation is not invertible the result will be invalid" — ours are invertible by construction |
+| 16 | `euler.rs hegel_euler_roundtrip_preserves_rotation` (`Quat` + `DQuat`) | `from_euler → to_euler → from_euler` preserves the rotation for **any** of the 24 orders and arbitrary finite radian angles (canonical ±q comparison) | Existing grid tests check the same roundtrip at 15° increments only; this fills the space between grid points and near the gimbal-lock poles |
+
+Generator notes (per the skill's generator discipline):
+
+- Bounds on float generators exist only to protect the tests' tolerance
+  models from intermediate overflow (|component| ≤ 1e18 where squares are
+  formed, ≤ 1e15 where quaternion sandwiches run, ≤ 1e3 for matrix elements
+  where triple products appear); each bound carries an in-source comment
+  saying it protects the error model, not a library contract.
+- The normalize-contract test uses the full f32 range *including NaN and
+  infinities*, with a magnitude-class generator (`extreme_triple`) that puts
+  all three components in the same class (any/tiny/huge) — independent draws
+  essentially never make `length_squared` underflow or overflow, and this
+  boundary-conjunction boost is what surfaced the pinned bug.
+- Unit quaternions/axes are constructed (draw in [-1,1]⁴, reject only
+  near-zero raw length, normalize) rather than filtered.
+- Angles are full-finite-range wherever the property is magnitude-independent
+  (axis-angle, euler roundtrip).
+- Euler order is drawn with `sampled_from` over all 24 `EulerRot` variants,
+  not a canned subset.
+
+## Test results
+
+Final committed state, default (NEON SIMD) build:
+**3439 passed; 1 failed** — the single failure is the deterministic pin of
+the real bug below (baseline before this work: 3417 passed, 0 failed; 23 new
+tests). With `--features scalar-math` (scalar backend): **3439 passed; 1
+failed** — the identical pin, since the buggy code is the shared scalar
+`Vec3` path.
+
+Run-and-Reflect checks performed:
+
+- **Liveness**: temporarily inverted the `+` assertion in
+  `hegel_vec3_vec3a_lanewise_ops_agree_exactly`; hegel reported a shrunk
+  counterexample (all six draws shrunk to `0.0`). Reverted, `.hegel/`
+  database deleted (and `.hegel/` added to `.gitignore`).
+- **Exploratory 10x runs**: all hegel tests were run at `test_cases = 1000`
+  five times with fresh random seeds on the default build and twice under
+  `--features scalar-math`, then reverted to the default count. One run
+  surfaced a tolerance-model error in my own length property (see Unsound
+  properties below), which was fixed; after the fix all 10x runs are green
+  apart from the pin.
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+### 1. `Vec3::try_normalize` returns `Some(v)` with `v.length() ≈ 1.07` for subnormal-length-squared inputs
+
+- **Test**: `hegel_pin_vec3_try_normalize_subnormal_length_not_unit`
+  (tests/vec3.rs, plain `#[test]` with the exact counterexample — marked
+  `KNOWN FAILURE`, fails deterministically on every run, both backends).
+- **Counterexample**: `Vec3::new(4e-23, 0.0, 0.0).try_normalize()` returns
+  `Some(Vec3(1.0685495, 0.0, 0.0))` — length 1.0685, while `is_normalized()`
+  documents a ~1e-4 threshold. Neither `None` nor unit length.
+- **Documented contract** (src/f32/vec3.rs): "Returns `self` normalized to
+  length 1.0 if possible, else returns `None`. In particular, if the input is
+  zero (or very close to zero), or non-finite, the result of this operation
+  will be `None`."
+- **Code path**: `try_normalize` computes `rcp = self.length_recip()` and
+  returns `Some(self * rcp)` whenever `rcp.is_finite() && rcp > 0.0`. For
+  `|v| ∈ [~2.5e-23, ~3.7e-21]`, `length_squared()` lands in the f32
+  *subnormal* range (quantum 1.4e-45), so it is quantized with huge relative
+  error — e.g. `(4e-23)² = 1.6e-45` rounds to `1.4e-45` — while `1/sqrt(ls)`
+  is still finite and positive. The guard admits the value; the result is off
+  by up to ~7% of unit length. `normalize_or_zero`/`normalize_or` share the
+  same guard and the same behavior (`Vec3A`, and by code inspection the
+  other float vector widths, likewise).
+- **Sibling handling**: the general property
+  `hegel_vec3_normalize_family_contract` excludes only the pinned region
+  (`0 < ls_f64 < 1e-37`, with a comment referencing the canonical test) and
+  still checks zero, huge, non-finite and ordinary inputs — it passes.
+- Not fixed, per instructions.
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+- **Purely relative tolerance for `length` vs the f64 oracle**: my initial
+  tolerance `8·eps·length` was violated (found in a 10x run) for
+  `Vec3(1.36e-20, 0, 0)` — not a glam divergence but the same subnormal
+  quantization as the pinned bug, amplified through `sqrt`: for subnormal
+  `length_squared` the absolute error is bounded by ~`sqrt(1.4e-45) ≈ 3.7e-23`
+  but is unboundedly large *relative* to the result. The committed tests add
+  an absolute `4e-23` term with a comment. (Kept as a tolerance-model fix
+  rather than an input exclusion, so the region stays covered.)
+- **Bitwise SIMD-vs-scalar equality for reductions (`dot`, `cross`,
+  `length`, inverses)**: glam only promises serialization/Display consistency
+  between backends, not bitwise arithmetic identity — SIMD backends may
+  reassociate or fuse (the NEON cross uses `vmlsq_f32`). Asserted exact
+  equality only for lane-wise ops (properties 1, 4) and calibrated tolerances
+  everywhere else.
+- **Exact `q * q.conjugate() == IDENTITY`**: the xyz cancellations are exact
+  only if no FMA contraction occurs; with SIMD fused ops an eps-scale residue
+  is legitimate. Used `32·eps` instead of exactness.
+- **`to_axis_angle` component-level roundtrip**: near the identity rotation
+  the axis is ill-conditioned (`acos(w)` for `w → 1` loses half the
+  significand, and the axis is `xyz / sin(θ/2)`), so
+  `from_axis_angle(to_axis_angle(q)) == q` with a tight component tolerance
+  is not a promise the code makes. Replaced by the axis-fixing and
+  matrix-agreement properties (10, 12), which are well-conditioned
+  everywhere.
+- **Grid-tolerance euler roundtrip (2e-6) on arbitrary angles**: the existing
+  grid tests' epsilon is only achievable away from the gimbal-lock poles;
+  extracting euler angles near a pole legitimately costs ~sqrt(eps). The
+  committed property uses 2e-3 (f32) / 1e-7 (f64), which still pins wrong
+  axis order or sign errors; a tighter bound would fail on legitimate
+  numerics, not bugs.
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+- The Oracle Sourcing section's "fast-path-vs-generic" pattern mapped
+  perfectly onto glam's twin types (`Vec3`/`Vec3A`, `Mat3`/`Mat3A`) — having
+  both backends in one build made the differential cheap. The
+  `--features scalar-math` rerun was nearly free extra coverage.
+- The boundary-conjunction advice ("some bugs require several drawn values to
+  hit boundaries simultaneously") was directly responsible for the real find:
+  per-component extreme draws never produced a subnormal `length_squared`;
+  the shared magnitude-class generator found it immediately.
+- The "keep a canonical deterministic pin, exclude the region from siblings"
+  guidance worked well; a plain `#[test]` with the exact input was the
+  cleanest deterministic pin.
+- One gap: the skill's tolerance guidance ("calibrated to magnitude") reads
+  as purely relative; a note that *subnormal intermediates make relative
+  tolerances unsound* (add an absolute floor derived from the subnormal
+  quantum) would have saved one 10x-run iteration — this exact issue appeared
+  twice here (once as a test-model bug, once as the real library bug).
